#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "bb7ae99a-b8d9-5e4d-906c-9e18d22c1404" :question/math-academy-id "e-8742" :question/problem "Calculate $\\nabla \\cdot (\\mathbf{F}-3\\mathbf{G})$ for the vector fields $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$, where\n$\\mathbf{F}=\\left(x^2+y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ \\left(xy-y^2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\quad \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{G}=x+y$." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot (k \\mathbf F) = k \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F$\n- $\\nabla \\cdot (\\mathbf F + \\mathbf G) = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F + \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (F - 3G) &= ∇ \\cdot (F) + ∇ \\cdot (-3G) \\\\\n&= ∇ \\cdot F - 3∇ \\cdot G\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F}$. In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy - y^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2x + x - 2y \\\\\n&= 3x - 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (F - 3G) &= ∇ \\cdot F - 3∇ \\cdot G \\\\\n&= (3x - 2y) - 3 \\cdot (x + y) \\\\\n&=-5y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179479 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7de054d4-8384-5aab-b2c4-a43b1f86a16c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8928" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac 1x, xy \\right \\rangle$ and $f(x,y) = \\dfrac 1{x}$, calculate $\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F) = f\\, \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}+ \\mathbf F \\cdot \\nabla f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}$ and $\\nabla f$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f &= ⟨ - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}, 0⟩, \\\\\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x}) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) =-\\frac{1}{x^{2}} + x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (fF) &= f∇ \\cdot F + F \\cdot ∇f \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x}(-\\frac{1}{x^{2}} + x) + ⟨\\frac{1}{x}, xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}, 0⟩ \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{3}} + 1 - \\frac{1}{x^{3}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{2}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc91159b-32f6-5b14-8204-3a2f6c722be1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xe^{2x} + z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177041} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5f894a-90fe-510d-a409-c5ca8688b739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xe^{2x} + xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177042} {:answer/id #uuid "33fd6630-5bf7-5db5-a03a-5b5ea227d3c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{2x} + z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177043} {:answer/id #uuid "67a7345a-91e3-564a-9338-d4512091a923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 + x)e^{2x} + xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177044} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec8451b-5ef8-5687-9400-f91661b3f7e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 + 2x)e^{2x} + 2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed5f894a-90fe-510d-a409-c5ca8688b739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xe^{2x} + xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177042} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d002358-95d4-582a-892b-c3379f81cba5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177040}] :question/id #uuid "208c1557-4948-59e8-ace1-5a9ccf87465a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105633" :question/problem "Calculate $∇ \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$F = xe^{2x}i - ye^{2x}j + xz^{2}k$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961763c8-d0c3-56e8-9575-f6490aa71907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x + 1)e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177047} {:answer/id #uuid "b921662f-633e-5895-9c5f-1939e5391bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x^{2} + 1)e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177048} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d96581-5c5f-591f-8a38-d7569d219bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x + 3)e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177049} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8eef4b-b617-5b8d-a0b5-915f2210b4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x + 1)e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177050} {:answer/id #uuid "14fb99cf-e4f3-5ede-bda2-9c2d4c84c58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x^{2} + 2x + 1)e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "961763c8-d0c3-56e8-9575-f6490aa71907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x + 1)e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177047} :answer-field/id #uuid "b676d0a0-d68a-5d10-b0e1-45a0d704b025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177046}] :question/id #uuid "61b57c2e-d378-5747-9b6d-7917cf190fff" :question/math-academy-id "q-105651" :question/problem "Given that $F(x, y, z) = ⟨e^{3x},-2ye^{3x}⟩$ and $f(x, y) = 2x$, calculate $∇ \\cdot (fF)$."}]}
